From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: GloriousEggroll <gloriouseggroll@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 00:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] kernelbase: Walk our own loader list directly through a real
 process handle.

NtReadVirtualMemory() only copies directly for the GetCurrentProcess()
pseudo-handle. With a real handle to our own process, every read is a
server round trip, and get_ldr_module() walks the loader list from its
head for each lookup. Enumerating all modules and asking for each name
with GetModuleFileNameEx() therefore costs a number of server calls that
grows with the square of the module count.

PunkBuster in Battlefield 4 does that with OpenProcess() on its own
process every few seconds, about 100 modules each time, and each scan
stalled the game for 50-75 ms.

The first read through the caller's handle still checks that it has
PROCESS_VM_READ access; only the walk after it uses the pseudo-handle.
The module name is still read through the caller's handle.

https://github.com/GloriousEggroll/proton-ge-custom/issues/984

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 dlls/kernelbase/debug.c | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/dlls/kernelbase/debug.c b/dlls/kernelbase/debug.c
--- a/dlls/kernelbase/debug.c
+++ b/dlls/kernelbase/debug.c
@@ -976,6 +976,11 @@
     if (!ReadProcessMemory( process, &pbi.PebBaseAddress->LdrData, &ldr_data, sizeof(ldr_data), NULL ))
         return FALSE;
 
+    /* A real handle to our own process still needs a server round trip for
+     * every read. The read above went through the caller's handle and so
+     * checked its access; walk our own loader list directly from here on. */
+    if (pbi.UniqueProcessId == GetCurrentProcessId()) process = GetCurrentProcess();
+
     /* This happens when running "old" wow64 configuration. Mark it as such. */
     if (!ldr_data)
     {
